WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.210
- [Narrator] On a normal evening in 2009,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.513
a man gets home from work
and boots up his computer.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.275
His name is Markus Persson,

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:12.693
but he often goes by
his screen name, Notch.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.476
Based in Stockholm, Sweden,

00:00:15.476 --> 00:00:19.050
Persson spends his evenings
developing passion projects,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.210
fun coding exercises that sometimes

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.030
end up as completed video games,

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:28.298
but more typically become
abandoned after a short time.

00:00:28.298 --> 00:00:29.608
But what he doesn't know

00:00:29.608 --> 00:00:32.250
is that the file he starts today

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.370
will change the gaming industry forever

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:39.930
and will make him one of the
wealthiest suites in the world.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.642
This is the making of "Minecraft."

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:46.225
(gentle electronic music)

00:00:47.097 --> 00:00:48.480
In the very beginning,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.310
the game that Persson was creating

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.650
wasn't called "Minecraft."

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.660
It was called "RubyDung,"

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.843
based on the two main
elements of the game,

00:00:56.843 --> 00:01:00.217
the Ruby Lands and the Dungeons.

00:01:00.217 --> 00:01:03.210
- [Markus] "It's going to
be a fantasy group manager.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.310
You don't actually do any quests yourself,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.310
but rather assemble,
equip and level up heroes,

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.850
and send among quests you've
gotten them the local town."

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.220
- [Narrator] Persson
created a texture atlas,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.440
which is essentially one large image file

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.110
that contains lots of smaller textures.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.590
Initially, this atlas only
contained two textures,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.750
grass and cobblestone.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:25.980
Using these elements,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.240
he created a digital
landscape filled with trees.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.321
Next up, Persson wanted to
implement a first person view

00:01:33.321 --> 00:01:36.171
of the heroes going on their quests,

00:01:36.171 --> 00:01:38.100
but quickly he realized

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.643
that it was becoming too
pixelated and blurry.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.085
Frustrated that he couldn't
get all of the features

00:01:46.085 --> 00:01:49.920
just quite right, Persson
scrapped the idea,

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.850
not realizing that he had just
created the first prototype

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.880
for what would later become "Minecraft."

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.871
One major limitation

00:01:57.871 --> 00:02:00.570
was that he could only work on his games

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:04.140
during the limited free
time after his day job.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.110
He worked as a programmer at Midas Player,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.640
the Swedish game developers

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.910
that would later go on
to create "Candy Crush."

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.520
However, his bosses at Midas Player

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.250
were unsupportive of Persson's hobby,

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.390
and once they discovered

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.240
that he was making his own
games in his free time,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.760
they felt that there
was a competing interest

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.333
and they barred him from
continuing making his own games.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.470
But Persson couldn't be stopped.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.286
Rather than comply, he
decided to quit his job

00:02:34.286 --> 00:02:38.160
in order to continue working
on his own passion projects,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.060
and he found a new job with
the software company jAlbum,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.720
where he befriended the CEO Carl Manneh.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.240
JAlbum was not a game studio,

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.720
but a company that made
digital photo album software.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.349
And Carl Manneh not only didn't mind

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:55.800
what Persson did in his free time,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.290
but instead he actively encouraged him

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.873
to continue designing games.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.810
At this point, RubyDung
still sat on the shelf,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.113
a scrap project with
little more development.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.890
That is until April, 2009

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.034
when Persson came across a new game

00:03:16.034 --> 00:03:18.930
that would change everything for him.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.177
The game was called "Infiniminer."

00:03:22.177 --> 00:03:24.480
"Infiniminer" is a multiplayer game

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.180
where players team up
against other players

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.360
to dig blocks in search of minerals.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.300
As blocks are dug up,
they can then be used

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.750
to create structures
to impede other teams.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.210
Though the objective of the game

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.360
was to mine up more minerals
than your opponents,

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.000
players quickly abandoned that goal

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.138
and instead realized it was much more fun

00:03:47.138 --> 00:03:49.143
to just build things.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.580
But despite the game's enormous potential,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.033
its development was
plagued with difficulties.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.590
Zachary Barth, the original
creator of "Infiniminer,"

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.920
had intended to create a
large network of players,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.180
but with the game's code

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.120
constantly being modified and rewritten,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.090
the game soon fragmented its user base

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.220
as the different versions of the game

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.980
weren't compatible with each other.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.970
Frustrated, Barth decided to
abandon the project as a whole

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.883
and the game's code became open source.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.980
The first time Persson
tried "Infiniminer,"

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.900
he immediately had an epiphany

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.480
that this was the game he wanted to do,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:38.040
and he had lots of ideas of
how he could make it better.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.610
He sat back down with the
previously abandoned "RubyDung"

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.200
and got to work tweaking it

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.710
into the new vision he had in his head.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:51.060
Reusing his existing textures,
Persson took a full weekend

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.600
to rework some of the
coding and art style.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.000
He called his creation "Cave Game"

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.680
and with just a little more work,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.195
it moved from just a test
to a working prototype.

00:05:02.195 --> 00:05:04.980
Now all he needed were players

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.463
and he had a plan for where he
could get some early testers.

00:05:10.638 --> 00:05:12.052
Just a few weeks prior,

00:05:12.052 --> 00:05:17.052
Persson joined an online indie
game forum called TIGSource.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:21.270
Here, developers can receive
feedback from other developers

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.013
on games they are creating.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:27.420
Persson began discussing
with others in the community

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.196
who quickly suggested a
new name for the project,

00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:34.500
"Minecraft: Order of the Stone,"

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.620
named for the two actions
you could do in the game,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.344
mining and crafting.

00:05:39.344 --> 00:05:43.413
Though Persson would later drop
the subtitle from the name.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.740
He then decided that this forum

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.440
would be the best place to
start getting early players

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.060
and have some immediate feedback

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.090
for how he could continue
developing the game.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.330
And so on May 17th, 2009,

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.190
he shared the first link

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.320
that would allow others in the forum

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.573
to begin play testing the game.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.465
- [Narrator 2] In "Minecraft,"
it's an alpha version,

00:06:07.465 --> 00:06:09.513
so there might be some crashes.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:14.070
- [Narrator] Just 12 minutes
after he posted the link,

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.927
the first person provides feedback.

00:06:16.927 --> 00:06:19.290
- [Player] "Oh hell, that's pretty cool.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.871
I just dig around on the ground a bit

00:06:20.871 --> 00:06:23.370
and suddenly I'm in this underground cave.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.437
Great sense of exploration already."

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.457
- [Narrator] Just three
minutes later, the next reply.

00:06:30.457 --> 00:06:32.370
- [Player 2] "The
animations are fantastic.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.086
It's generally really
derivative at the moment

00:06:34.086 --> 00:06:35.900
of 'Infiniminer.'"

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.620
- [Narrator] Quickly, more and
more people are downloading,

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:44.373
providing feedback and above all, praise.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.640
And though the forum
was made for feedback,

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.090
players couldn't resist posting
the things they have made.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.856
And since people were
showing off screenshots

00:06:52.856 --> 00:06:54.420
of their creations,

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.623
more and more people were
encouraged to jump in

00:06:56.623 --> 00:06:59.520
and give it a try themselves.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.890
The community also had plenty of feedback

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:05.100
for features that could make
"Minecraft" even better,

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.300
and Persson was on it, updating
his blog nearly every day

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:10.920
with the progress he was making

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.563
on adding many of the ideas presented.

00:07:15.917 --> 00:07:18.420
- [Markus] "I plan on
having several game modes,

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:23.157
creative, survival, team
survival, a fortress."

00:07:23.998 --> 00:07:25.670
- [Narrator] Though
players were consistently

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:29.280
finding the gameplay
addicting in this early stage,

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.430
there was no way to save
what they had built.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.454
There was also no way to play with friends

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:38.013
or to play on maps that
others had created.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.091
Persson would often sit
down with the intention

00:07:41.091 --> 00:07:45.090
to add these commonly requested
elements to "Minecraft,"

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:47.610
but he would repeatedly get distracted

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:50.550
due to the challenges of
implementing multiplayer

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:54.487
and instead would focus
on more fun features.

00:07:54.487 --> 00:07:57.693
- [Markus] "Okay, multiplayer
was boring, but here's water.

00:07:59.061 --> 00:08:01.083
Should 'Minecraft' have blood?

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.647
No, blood is overused."

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.430
- [Narrator] Next up
was adding multiplayer,

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.380
which would take quite some time.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.290
But Persson was determined
to get it up and running,

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:17.280
though not without some more
side projects along the way

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.957
and more challenges to overcome.

00:08:19.957 --> 00:08:23.403
- [Markus] "So I didn't do
multiplayer code, but I had fun.

00:08:24.424 --> 00:08:27.630
Multiplayer will be in the
form of a downloadable server,

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.250
which will broadcast its IP

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.173
to the main server when it starts up.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.120
Ugh, I feel stupid.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.657
Server admins could host any data

00:08:37.657 --> 00:08:39.540
they want on their servers.

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:42.723
This means anything, including bad things.

00:08:45.649 --> 00:08:48.600
Multiplayer's starting to go good.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.090
I've got player connect/disconnect going

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:52.917
and map streaming on login."

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.220
- [Narrator] With the
release of multiplayer,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.960
nearly a month had passed

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.680
of constant updates,
fine-tuning and debugging

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.219
since "Minecraft" was first introduced

00:09:03.219 --> 00:09:06.660
to the TIGSource community.

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:07.680
And Persson realized

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.546
that his passion project
could be much more

00:09:09.546 --> 00:09:12.630
than just a game he was making as a hobby.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:15.360
If it was going to
continue to grow like this,

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.263
it was time to put a price
tag on it and go commercial.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:23.430
Though today, it may
seem completely normal

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:26.010
that an indie developer sells their game

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.260
rather than releasing it for free,

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.950
this wasn't actually the
case in the late 2000s.

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:33.780
The expected practice rather,

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:35.970
was to release a game as freeware

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.790
and to make money through the use of ads.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:42.000
Persson, however, came
up with a different plan.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.770
Having seen how valuable

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:46.170
the direct feedback
from the forum has been,

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:49.593
he designed his pricing
to reward early adopters.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:51.990
And despite the warning

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:54.540
that people wouldn't pay for such a game,

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:57.783
sales started coming
in almost immediately.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:02.520
Persson even started seeing
such a high influx in his income

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.123
that his PayPal account was
frozen due to suspected fraud.

00:10:07.242 --> 00:10:09.480
It was also enough that he was able

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:13.830
to change his status from
full-time to part-time at jAlbum,

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:15.810
allowing him to spend even more time

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.407
on developing "Minecraft."

00:10:18.407 --> 00:10:22.590
Month after month, Persson
followed the same process,

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:24.889
listening to feedback from
those who were playing,

00:10:24.889 --> 00:10:28.290
implementing more features
that were commonly requested

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:31.170
and fixing any bugs that were reported.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:34.080
He also during this
time met another person

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:35.820
on the TIGSource forum

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.643
that could help him with
the game's sound design.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:44.280
Daniel Rosenfeld is a
musician and sound engineer,

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.197
and he began to collaborate with Persson

00:10:46.197 --> 00:10:49.230
to provide sound effects for "Minecraft"

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.080
as well as produce the iconic soundtrack

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:54.153
that is still used to this day.

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:01.119
By 2010, sales of "Minecraft"
had grown to the point

00:11:01.119 --> 00:11:03.537
where Persson was able
to quit his part-time job

00:11:03.537 --> 00:11:05.880
with jAlbum altogether.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:09.090
And it was clear that as
"Minecraft" continued to expand,

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.640
Persson would need an
actual business structure

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.833
to handle the explosive growth.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.620
But since he was uninterested

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.780
in actually running a business,

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.750
he decided to call in some help.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.774
First was a friend and colleague
from Persson's old days

00:11:24.774 --> 00:11:28.143
at Midas Player, Jakob Porser.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:32.160
Second was yet another colleague,

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:37.110
but this time from jAlbum,
the former CEO Carl Manneh.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:39.780
Together, the three would
establish the company

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:43.203
that would officially
back "Minecraft," Mojang.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.550
Persson was no longer working from home,

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.070
but had built a small team.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.015
The game was still not
officially finished,

00:11:52.015 --> 00:11:56.373
but it already became immensely
popular amongst players.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:59.368
And by August of 2010,

00:11:59.368 --> 00:12:01.796
Minecraft had grown enough
to catch the attention

00:12:01.796 --> 00:12:06.240
of other much larger game
developing companies,

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:07.743
such as Valve.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:11.382
Persson was invited for
meeting to the headquarters

00:12:11.382 --> 00:12:14.580
of the gaming giant in
Bellevue, Washington,

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:18.060
and he accepted the meeting
halfway across the world.

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.397
And while there, Persson
thought he could also meet

00:12:20.397 --> 00:12:22.323
some "Minecraft" fans.

00:12:24.607 --> 00:12:28.590
- [Markus] "I have all day on
August 31st free in Bellevue

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:31.680
if you guys could organize
something on the forums,

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.400
and I figured we could
call it MinecraftCon 2010,

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.081
even if it's just three
people sitting around

00:12:37.081 --> 00:12:40.047
in uncomfortable silence for 20 minutes."

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:42.810
- [Narrator] During
the meeting with Valve,

00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:45.840
the company offered Persson a position.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:48.840
However, he decided to turn the offer down

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.860
as his focus was solely on Minecraft.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:54.810
After these negotiations, however,

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.923
Persson joined a small gathering of people

00:12:56.923 --> 00:13:00.303
that had come around
because of his blog post.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.170
A little more than 50 people turned up

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.243
to hang out in a park,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:12.140
a meetup that would go down as
the very first MinecraftCon,

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.028
and this spontaneous event

00:13:15.028 --> 00:13:18.270
would give the Mojang team an idea.

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:19.310
By the end of 2010,

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:23.100
"Minecraft" had reached
over 700,000 sales,

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.727
and yet by this point,

00:13:24.727 --> 00:13:28.380
"Minecraft" was still
technically not a finished game.

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:31.080
It was still in its beta phase,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:32.610
but Mojang's development team

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:36.000
was close to finishing the actual release.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:39.390
With so much hype already
built around the game,

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:41.344
Mojang knew they needed some fanfare

00:13:41.344 --> 00:13:44.220
to go along with the game's launch.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.096
And in November, 2011,

00:13:46.096 --> 00:13:49.653
they decided to host an official MineCon.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.379
This time, instead of an
impromptu gathering of 50 people,

00:13:56.379 --> 00:14:01.379
it would be a sold out organized
event with 4,500 attendees.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:05.340
And in quintessential early 2010s fashion,

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:09.383
this Minecraft convention
had a party DJ'd by Deadmau5.

00:14:09.383 --> 00:14:12.050
(bouncy music)

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:17.132
All of this would be leading
up to the grand finale

00:14:17.132 --> 00:14:19.770
of MineCon 2011,

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.290
the official release of the game.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:25.050
As the Mojang team gathered on stage,

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:28.000
Persson would stand by
a large blocky lever,

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:32.520
and with one pull, "Minecraft"
was officially launched

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.053
as a finished project.

00:14:39.552 --> 00:14:41.499
Even though the game was now finished,

00:14:41.499 --> 00:14:44.601
the team would continue working
on it, releasing updates,

00:14:44.601 --> 00:14:48.704
and adding new features even until today.

00:14:48.704 --> 00:14:51.920
But for a Persson himself,
it was the end of a chapter.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:54.478
After two years of continuous
work on "Minecraft,"

00:14:54.478 --> 00:14:57.211
he was ready to move on to other projects

00:14:57.211 --> 00:15:01.740
in order to get back to what
he loved doing most, coding.

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.270
And so just two weeks

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:05.190
after "Minecraft"'s official release,

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.890
Persson decided to step
down from his position

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:10.107
as lead developer of "Minecraft."

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:15.127
With Jens Bergensten now at its helm,

00:15:15.127 --> 00:15:17.790
"Minecraft" continued to flourish,

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:21.210
but Persson didn't get the
rest he was hoping for.

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.760
Still perceived as the
maker of "Minecraft,"

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.366
players continued to send any issues

00:15:25.366 --> 00:15:27.510
they encountered to him,

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:29.763
and he was flooded by complaints.

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:31.493
For a long time,

00:15:31.493 --> 00:15:33.630
he would attempt to ignore
the negative feedback

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:35.130
that he could do nothing about

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:39.243
until it finally came to a head in 2014.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.590
As the popularity of
"Minecraft" continued exploding,

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:46.920
an inevitable feature from
players began to emerge,

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:48.600
pay to win.

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:51.166
Mojang has always allowed
public servers to be created

00:15:51.166 --> 00:15:52.890
so that players around the world

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.230
can play the same map together.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.440
However, this opened a door
for some to take advantage

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:00.292
and create servers where players

00:16:00.292 --> 00:16:03.840
have to spend money to gain an advantage.

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:05.580
These pay to win services

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:08.970
violated the end user license
agreement of "Minecraft,"

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:11.550
which is why in a controversial move,

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:14.310
Mojang began to crack
down on these servers

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:19.310
and gave them a deadline to
either adapt or to shut down.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:22.260
Following this statement by Mojang,

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.930
there was immense negative feedback.

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:27.990
And though Persson was no
longer working on "Minecraft,"

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:30.720
he still received the
bulk of the criticism.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:35.720
In frustration, he sent out
a tweet on June 17th, 2014,

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:39.183
that would change the
direction of Mojang forever.

00:16:40.687 --> 00:16:42.510
- [Markus] "Anyone want
to buy my share of Mojang

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:44.400
so I can move on with my life?

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.290
Getting hate for trying
to do the right thing

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:47.547
is not my gig."

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:49.680
- [Narrator] The very next day,

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.577
Manneh began fielding calls from companies

00:16:51.577 --> 00:16:54.660
that wanted to know if
Persson was serious,

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:59.460
including studio giants EA,
Activision and Microsoft.

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:01.110
And after giving it a thought,

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:02.839
Persson realized he actually was serious

00:17:02.839 --> 00:17:04.890
about selling Mojang

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:08.768
so that he could finally
distance himself completely.

00:17:08.768 --> 00:17:13.530
And after several rounds of
negotiations, a deal was struck.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.367
- Mojang has confirmed it's
being sold to Microsoft

00:17:15.367 --> 00:17:17.193
for $2.5 billion.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:20.040
- [Narrator] The deal with Microsoft

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.527
meant that the Mojang
founders Persson, Manneh,

00:17:22.527 --> 00:17:25.260
and Porser would step away completely,

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:28.683
whereas all of their employees
would be barred from layoffs.

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:34.170
The Microsoft deal was worth
a staggering $2.5 billion.

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:36.450
And as the major shareholder of Mojang,

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:39.333
Persson had become a
billionaire overnight.

00:17:41.117 --> 00:17:44.670
(upbeat electronic music)

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:45.900
This is the part of the script

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.600
that should have been
the easiest to write.

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.690
After all, it's a classic happy ending.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:54.000
Persson had achieved the unthinkable.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.490
He turned his passion
project started at home

00:17:56.490 --> 00:18:00.033
into an empire, becoming
wealthier than he ever imagined.

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:02.070
In the following years,

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:04.410
Persson would continue developing games

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:06.870
and he would do what billionaires do,

00:18:06.870 --> 00:18:08.310
moving to Los Angeles

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:10.003
and buying the most expensive mansion

00:18:10.003 --> 00:18:14.433
ever built in Beverly Hills,
hosting extravagant parties.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:18.450
Meanwhile, "Minecraft"
continues to thrive.

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.850
With over 300 million sales,

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:24.723
it is to this day the most
sold video game ever made.

00:18:27.519 --> 00:18:30.630
But yet, this is the part
of the script for this video

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:32.883
that was by far the hardest to write.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:38.296
In 2017, a series of racist
and homophobic tweets

00:18:38.296 --> 00:18:42.360
led to Microsoft distancing
itself from Persson.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:44.250
They scrubbed his name from the game

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:45.720
and did not invite him

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:49.663
to the 10th anniversary
celebration of "Minecraft."

00:18:50.614 --> 00:18:54.330
Persson's tweets were bad, really bad,

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:56.310
and they have significantly impacted

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:58.353
his legacy in recent years.

00:19:03.327 --> 00:19:06.330
For me, the hard part
about writing this section

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:09.227
is figuring out how to talk
about someone's harmful comments

00:19:09.227 --> 00:19:12.180
without making things worse.

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:13.260
In situations like this,

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:16.323
it often feels like there are
two paths someone can take.

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:18.390
The easy path is the one

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:20.850
where they double down
on their harmful views,

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:23.130
find a crowd that supports them.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.110
But then there is the hard path,

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:26.846
acknowledging the mistakes,
learning from them,

00:19:26.846 --> 00:19:29.013
and making an effort to change.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:31.207
And the easier we make it for people

00:19:31.207 --> 00:19:35.167
to take this second path,
the more might take it.

00:19:35.167 --> 00:19:38.700
Markus Persson's story
is undeniably inspiring.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:40.595
There are few stories that
highlight as well as this

00:19:40.595 --> 00:19:42.937
how powerful a good idea is

00:19:42.937 --> 00:19:45.286
and how much creativity can flourish

00:19:45.286 --> 00:19:47.253
within constraints as his.

00:19:48.198 --> 00:19:50.506
And more than anything,
he has created a world

00:19:50.506 --> 00:19:53.493
that I've always experienced
as incredibly inclusive.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:58.053
When is "Minecraft" the most fun?

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:00.470
Is it when you open a new world,

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:04.110
starting to build
something new from scratch?

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.270
Or is it when you've built an empire

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:09.153
and when you have an abundance
of everything there is?

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:14.178
Markus Persson is now in
that second stage of life.

00:20:14.178 --> 00:20:16.928
(mouse clicking)

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:21.210
When I first started this channel,

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:22.560
I never could have imagined

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.950
it would lead to making videos like this.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:28.485
I got to hire an actor, build out a set,

00:20:28.485 --> 00:20:31.110
and even rent a sound stage

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:34.380
in order to make this story come to life.

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:36.300
And over the course of the last years,

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:40.440
I have produced my most
